WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.239
Hello, everybody. So a while ago, our CEO went onto Amazon and then he typed the cheapest

2
00:00:12.239 --> 00:00:17.719
laptop he could find. And he just ordered the laptop and he brought it to the office.

3
00:00:17.719 --> 00:00:23.120
So he brought it to the office. It was an Acer Celeron laptop, really low spec, very,

4
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.959
very similar to a Chromebook, and he decided to test Postman on this really, really cheap

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:35.040
laptop. We were not surprised that it was painful to watch. It was very, very, very

6
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.400
difficult to get Postman working on that laptop. It was very slow. Sometimes it takes

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.000
about 30, 40, 50 seconds to launch, and it will just start crashing. The CPU starts

8
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.599
spiking. And it was a very difficult thing to see. And we knew we had a performance

9
00:00:53.599 --> 00:01:01.319
problem. But only then we realized how bad the problem was. Hi, everybody. So my name

10
00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:06.919
is Ruben Casas, I am a staff engineer at Postman, and today I'm going to show you five tips

11
00:01:06.919 --> 00:01:11.680
on how to improve your app performance. From picking the right metric, so understanding

12
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.559
your problem, to reviewing and creating an action plan to bring the company together

13
00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:22.080
and solve the performance of your app. Doesn't matter if it's a mobile app, a desktop app,

14
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.639
or a React app, you should be able to use these tips to get up and going and improving

15
00:01:26.639 --> 00:01:35.400
the performance like we did with Postman. So where do we start? In performance, the

16
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:42.559
only place to start is measure, measure, measure. You need to start measuring performance. You

17
00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:48.400
cannot start solving a problem unless you start measuring performance. And then after

18
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.139
you measure performance, the first thing you need to do is to pick a north star metric.

19
00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:59.400
You can measure multiple things, a lot of things, but you need to pick one metric and

20
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:06.120
start obsessing over that metric and fixing and improving that metric. So we already had

21
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.320
the metrics, some metrics at Postman, we had the usual stuff, we had the Core Vitals, the

22
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.279
Lighthouse metrics. But to solve this problem, because it was so complicated, we knew we

23
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.080
had to go deeper to understand the problem. So which one? Which one do we choose from

24
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.699
Lighthouse? Which metric? We decided to go a different way. I found recently this concept

25
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:35.880
called TTF. And TTF is used in mobile gaming. And as you can see in the Super Mario GIF

26
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:43.199
here, is the time it takes to have fun. So from the moment you start a Mario game, you

27
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.639
immediately start having fun. You start jumping, killing enemies, getting money, getting

28
00:02:47.639 --> 00:02:52.740
power-ups. So they have this term in mobile gaming called time to fun. Make sure that as

29
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:59.399
soon as people open their app, they start having fun. Now our application is not a

30
00:02:59.399 --> 00:03:04.440
mobile app that you can use, but I got inspired by that and decided to choose a

31
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:11.919
different metric called TTU. And TTU stands for Time to Usability. So Time to Usability

32
00:03:11.919 --> 00:03:16.600
is a metric that we have chosen to improve the performance of our application. And what is

33
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.240
Time to Usability? Well, there are examples of other applications that are using this

34
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.520
metric. They don't call it TTU. And TTU is a term that I came up with. But some other

35
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.199
companies like VS Code have a concept of Time to Usability. And for them, Time to

36
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:37.119
Usability is the time that takes for a user to get a blinking cursor and start coding

37
00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:42.160
on VS Code. Other companies like Slack, for example, they have the same, which is how

38
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:50.080
fast until the user can open a chat and send a message. Now, what about TTI, Time to

39
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.279
Interactive? We already have a metric in Lighthouse called Time to Interactive. And

40
00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:59.639
this Time to Interactive metric is measuring the time that it takes the application for

41
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:04.839
you to be able to click and do things around it. But the problem with TTI is, in

42
00:04:04.839 --> 00:04:09.320
short, that it's technically ready. So your application is ready from the technical

43
00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.800
sense, so it's not blocked, you can start doing things. However, it doesn't tell you

44
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.440
when you can actually start getting benefits from your app. In this case, TTU is

45
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.959
probably a better metric because it reflects the real usability and the real things that

46
00:04:23.959 --> 00:04:28.679
developers are doing with your application. And we can optimize for what actually

47
00:04:28.679 --> 00:04:36.359
matters to our users. So we selected our TTU, Time to Usability, and the Time to Usability

48
00:04:36.359 --> 00:04:40.679
for Postman was the time that it takes for the Workbench, which is the main workspace

49
00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:45.559
where you perform most of the operations in Postman, and for the main part of the

50
00:04:45.559 --> 00:04:51.640
application, which is sending requests to be active. We put this as our critical path.

51
00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:56.200
The Time to Usability was the time that it took for someone to click onto the desktop

52
00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:01.720
application icon for this page to render and for that button to be ready for you to

53
00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.040
send a request, which is what most of the Postman users do exactly when they start the

54
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:16.040
application. So we've chosen our TTU and we've defined it. Now, how do we measure it? Because

55
00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.519
we can just say, this is the time it takes for Postman to launch and show the button,

56
00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:26.760
but we need real data to measure this. And as I mentioned, TTU is a made-up metric that I came

57
00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.760
up with. It's not something that's provided with Lighthouse or with the Core Web Vitals.

58
00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:36.359
So we needed to measure this. And the way we did this was we inserted marks for the platform

59
00:05:36.359 --> 00:05:41.880
initialization, JavaScript evaluation, data fetching, rendering. Then we also captured the

60
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.959
navigation resources. How long does it take for you to download that JavaScript, if it's coming

61
00:05:45.959 --> 00:05:51.079
from the network or from the service worker? We turned this into spans and we pushed them into

62
00:05:51.079 --> 00:05:57.160
our RAM and our synthetic labs for tracking. And our users are everywhere in the world.

63
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:02.679
There are different CPUs, different laptops. So we had to get segmented dashboards per CPU

64
00:06:02.679 --> 00:06:07.160
and region just to see how the variation of the performance was between those regions and

65
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:14.679
different users. You can do this. You can measure with just a built-in browser API,

66
00:06:14.679 --> 00:06:19.959
performance.mark. So performance.mark is very powerful, which is basically, you add it to your code

67
00:06:19.959 --> 00:06:25.799
and you start measuring from the moment you put a marker until the next part you put this marker,

68
00:06:25.799 --> 00:06:31.720
it will measure the time that it takes. So for example, in this case, this is a pseudocode

69
00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.320
of what we started measuring. We measured from the time that the platform initialized,

70
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.079
the time that it took for the JavaScript bundles to load and execute and parse and compile,

71
00:06:41.079 --> 00:06:46.519
the time that it took for our data fetching to get all that data to show to the user,

72
00:06:46.519 --> 00:06:52.279
and the time that it took for that render of the critical UI to show on the page. And at the end,

73
00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:58.600
we just marked this with, okay, this core feature is ready and our time to usability is ready for us.

74
00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:08.200
Now, how do you see this locally and in synthetic monitoring? You can do this by going to the

75
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:12.679
performance in the developer tools, you run a performance profile on your laptop,

76
00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:17.559
and these markers will start appearing in the timings section of that performance profile.

77
00:07:18.519 --> 00:07:23.880
Now, we don't want to just do this test on our laptops, we want to make sure that we can measure

78
00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:29.000
on real users and real user devices. So even though that we can do this locally,

79
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:34.920
we also wanted to aggregate and publish these to our real user metrics. So we use another API called

80
00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:41.320
the performance.measure that gathers all of those performance markers that we showed you a minute

81
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:47.399
ago. And you can take those markers and then send them to your analytic service. In this case,

82
00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:52.359
I'm just console logging this, but you can just create an API that sends this information to your

83
00:07:52.359 --> 00:08:01.160
real user metrics system. Now, this will allow you to create a lot of dashboards, and we created

84
00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:07.799
a lot of dashboards. We have segmentations, how many users were seeing the application critical

85
00:08:07.799 --> 00:08:13.959
path in under 10 seconds, how many people were seeing under 20, 30 seconds. We segmented this by

86
00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:19.959
user, device, operating system, the different type of postman application, if it was web, desktop,

87
00:08:19.959 --> 00:08:29.640
or if it was on Windows or macOS. Now, we set our goal. Our goal was to reduce the time to usability

88
00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:37.559
from 35 to 40 seconds that was taken at P90 to 10 seconds. And why we decided to choose 10 seconds?

89
00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:43.320
Well, we did some research, and 10 seconds is the maximum time that someone will wait for something

90
00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:49.400
to happen before they get distracted and go and do something else. So 10 seconds was our benchmark.

91
00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:54.760
And even though we had a lot of devices that were launching under 10 seconds, some of them were like

92
00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:01.159
two, three, five seconds, but those were like the super powerful Macs that would run the application.

93
00:09:01.159 --> 00:09:07.239
And we wanted to target every single device, even the low-powered devices to run under 10 seconds.

94
00:09:07.239 --> 00:09:13.320
So this is our goal. We've chosen this as our goal for the TTU. So this is what it takes us to our

95
00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:20.760
takeaway number one. Pick that no-start metric and then obsess on this metric until you achieve it.

96
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:29.320
Improve it, measure it, obsess, and then go for it. So what's next? We picked our metric.

97
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:33.479
We measured our metric. So now what we need to do is we need to understand the problem.

98
00:09:34.039 --> 00:09:38.840
We knew we had a big performance problem, but we didn't know what was causing that problem.

99
00:09:40.039 --> 00:09:45.400
So the big million-dollar question is, why is our app so slow? Why does it take so long to start?

100
00:09:46.119 --> 00:09:50.919
We took all of those measurements and we divided the application launch time into different sections

101
00:09:50.919 --> 00:09:57.239
that will allow us to understand where that time was going. So in the first part, we realized that

102
00:09:57.239 --> 00:10:03.479
it was about 22% of the time I was taking about Electron and the main process of Electron,

103
00:10:03.479 --> 00:10:10.840
just all that process starting up. Then our web view will start downloading, parsing JavaScript

104
00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:18.200
and compiling it. And that was taking about 40 seconds. Then the workspace, rendering all the

105
00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:23.559
API calls, the data fetching and rendering was taking the remainder all the time. So this way,

106
00:10:23.559 --> 00:10:27.960
we can visualize and segment the different parts of the application and then focus on

107
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:33.640
those different problems. The takeaway number two is you cannot solve a problem that you don't

108
00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.719
understand. So first, we need to understand the problem and you need to start measuring

109
00:10:37.719 --> 00:10:45.000
progress towards fixing that problem. So what now? We know what the problem is. We have measured

110
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.440
the problem. What do we need to do? We need to come up with a plan. So we needed a plan of action.

111
00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:54.919
And the first thing that we did was, okay, what have we done so far? What thing has worked? What

112
00:10:54.919 --> 00:11:00.280
things have not worked? And most importantly, what's the most low-hanging fruit, the obvious

113
00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:05.960
things that we can do to improve the performance of our application. So there was an obvious section

114
00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.479
that stood out and it was 40 percent of our application was downloading, parsing and compiling

115
00:11:11.479 --> 00:11:17.000
JavaScript. There was a huge part, nearly 50 percent of the time was in this section, which is basically

116
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:22.039
even before we start rendering, our application was taking this much time, but just downloading

117
00:11:22.039 --> 00:11:29.400
and parsing JavaScript. Our bundle size was insanely huge. It was 14 megabytes of minified

118
00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:33.960
gzip compressed JavaScript. And this is the representation of what that looked like and all

119
00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:39.400
the code that we were shipping up front. And I know what you're thinking. 14 megabytes of JavaScript,

120
00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:47.559
are you insane? Well, there's an explanation for this. Postman app was a single page

121
00:11:47.559 --> 00:11:52.840
application that was a part of an Electron bundle that was served inside the bundle. So if you're

122
00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:57.799
familiar with Electron, you can get all the JavaScript files and then just ship it with the

123
00:11:57.799 --> 00:12:02.840
Electron bundle so you don't need to download anything from the network. But after years of

124
00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:09.559
iteration, circular dependencies, we ended up with a big, massive application with a lot of code.

125
00:12:09.559 --> 00:12:15.400
And we didn't have the need for code splitting or lazy loading because everything was inside

126
00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:21.799
an Electron bundle so you didn't have to pay that network cost. Now, after that, we moved to

127
00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:28.840
the web and we started shipping updates over the air. So we started downloading that JavaScript

128
00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:34.599
from our CDN instead of the Electron bundle to allow us to do updates faster. So we didn't have

129
00:12:34.599 --> 00:12:40.679
to download the Electron bundle every single time. But as you can imagine, that meant that now

130
00:12:40.679 --> 00:12:46.840
instead of having the 14 megabytes of JavaScript inside Electron, we had to download 14 megabytes

131
00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:52.039
over the network, which was a bit crazy. And that's the reason why our application was so big.

132
00:12:53.479 --> 00:12:58.760
I created this visualization. I went to the webpack stats and then tried to create a visualization

133
00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:05.799
of what our module graph and our bundle looked like. I ended up with this crazy, cool gif of

134
00:13:05.799 --> 00:13:11.640
our bundle, which I call the Supernova Module Graph. I remember I crashed my laptop a few times

135
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:17.159
just trying to render this. It was impossible to understand all the connections and all the

136
00:13:17.159 --> 00:13:21.640
things that happened in our module graph that made that 14 megabytes of JavaScript bundle.

137
00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:28.039
So the question is, this is very obvious. It is big. It's a lot of JavaScript. But people might be

138
00:13:28.039 --> 00:13:35.960
asking, does the bundle size matter? And actually, it does. We did some research and

139
00:13:36.599 --> 00:13:43.559
the JavaScript, even if you compile it, if you compress it into Gzip, it will take

140
00:13:44.119 --> 00:13:51.239
a long time for the browser to execute it. So it's not the same as having a massive JPEG image.

141
00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:57.719
The bundle size, the JavaScript execution, takes a toll on your application, not just from the

142
00:13:57.719 --> 00:14:03.719
download part, from the network transfer, but also the parsing and the compiling. So if you're asking,

143
00:14:03.719 --> 00:14:09.479
does the bundle size matter? Yes, it does. And this is where we wanted to start reducing that bundle.

144
00:14:11.239 --> 00:14:17.080
We started applying all the Performance 101 optimizations, the basic stuff, the things

145
00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:21.400
that you learned that you have to do. So we started splitting the application into smaller

146
00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:26.440
chunks using lazy loading and doing some code splitting, ensuring that we just load the parts

147
00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:31.239
that were needed for the critical path. And we also introduced a service worker caching.

148
00:14:31.239 --> 00:14:37.479
So every time the application is launched, we cache all of the assets onto the service worker,

149
00:14:37.479 --> 00:14:41.880
so you don't have to download them from the network again. And when there is an update,

150
00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:46.280
we cache those assets in the background. So when you open the application, you don't even

151
00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:50.840
notice that the application has downloaded anything from the network, because it's already

152
00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:55.960
pre-cached into the service worker. But that was not enough and we had to go deeper.

153
00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:02.919
And I found a tool that I considered was the tipping point that helped us solve this

154
00:15:02.919 --> 00:15:07.239
problem. And the tool was called a StatusScope. And there is a new tool called Iris Doctor,

155
00:15:07.239 --> 00:15:12.440
which is very similar. I recommend you taking a look into it, which helps you understand

156
00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:18.200
that super NOA model graph and understand how you can start pulling things apart from that.

157
00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:24.599
The bundle analyzer has this very powerful feature. So StatusScope shows you, for example,

158
00:15:24.599 --> 00:15:30.200
in this case, why lodash is being included into the bundle. And now lodash is a really big

159
00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:34.280
dependency. And that was the first thing that we did, was like, okay, who is using lodash and why

160
00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:42.200
is lodash being bundled up front? And it tells you what file, what line is importing this module,

161
00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:46.840
so you can go and lazy load it or just completely remove it, which is what we recommend with lodash.

162
00:15:48.599 --> 00:15:55.320
So the results were incredible. We went from 14 megabytes of JavaScript compiled minified gzip

163
00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:03.080
to under 2.7 megabytes. There was still a lot of room for improvement, but Postman is nearly

164
00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:07.960
like a code editor. So we have a lot of things like the Monaco editor, which are really,

165
00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:12.840
really big and we cannot just remove. But that was huge. That was an incredible

166
00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.440
reduction of bundle from 14 megabytes to 2.7 megabytes.

167
00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:22.119
One thing that we made sure that we introduced was, okay, we need to make sure that we don't

168
00:16:22.119 --> 00:16:27.880
regress. That we don't, after reducing the bundle size in such a manner, we don't want people to go

169
00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:33.159
and add a new module that is going just to undo all the work that we have done. So we decided

170
00:16:33.159 --> 00:16:38.359
to introduce PR blocking checks on every single pull request. It will give you a status code

171
00:16:38.359 --> 00:16:44.280
report that will tell you if you are increasing the bundle size and if you are impacting the

172
00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:50.679
critical path on our metric. So we introduced this and we started to implement the PR checks,

173
00:16:50.679 --> 00:16:57.000
so we didn't go back to where we started. Now, the next thing was improving the network calls.

174
00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:01.559
And if you're a front-end engineer, you might be thinking, well, all of the optimizations are in

175
00:17:01.559 --> 00:17:07.479
the code, but actually some optimizations happen at the network level. We had so many network calls.

176
00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:13.880
There were hundreds of, for example, LaunchDarkly feature flags that were dead and we wanted to

177
00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:18.760
remove them. So that's the place we started. We started by removing LaunchDarkly flags,

178
00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:25.239
removing all those network calls, and we started prefetching the data. We took the critical path

179
00:17:25.239 --> 00:17:30.280
API calls that were required for us to render. And we started prefetching them upfront,

180
00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:35.479
even before your JavaScript started executing. So the data was already in parallel from the

181
00:17:35.479 --> 00:17:41.400
beginning, ready for you to use. And we also looked at the back-end. As front-end developers,

182
00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:47.319
we like to think we can do a lot of micro optimizations here and there, but sometimes

183
00:17:47.319 --> 00:17:52.439
the back-end is the one that is taking the longest and you need to go and speak to the back-end team

184
00:17:52.439 --> 00:17:56.439
and say, okay, we need to improve the back-end performance of these APIs that are used.

185
00:17:56.439 --> 00:17:59.880
And you'll be surprised how much performance you can gain from just that.

186
00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:09.400
Now, the last thing was this 22% that we found, and it was the electron performance. And you might

187
00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:13.160
be thinking, well, this is electron. There's nothing that you can do there to improve it.

188
00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:18.119
Well, we were wrong. It was the electron section, so it was before the WebView was launched, and it

189
00:18:18.119 --> 00:18:23.319
was all pure electron overhead. But then we realized that there were some things that we could improve

190
00:18:23.319 --> 00:18:29.719
for the electron performance. So the first thing was, let's improve the startup overhead of

191
00:18:29.719 --> 00:18:35.640
electron. Remove any database, bootstrap, initialization, blocking scripts that are happening

192
00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:42.520
on our code base that we can just remove and remove the blocking. And also, we started bundling

193
00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:48.119
the main process code. So in electron, one of the recommendations is to use a bundler and actually

194
00:18:48.119 --> 00:18:54.839
bundle the code, the JavaScript, that runs in electron. And also, something that we found that

195
00:18:54.839 --> 00:19:01.079
was very performance intensive was the require calls. Every time that you require a node module

196
00:19:01.079 --> 00:19:06.520
inside the electron main process, that was taking a toll on the total performance and the launch

197
00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:12.680
initialization. So for us, it was just start removing unnecessary modules and necessary dependencies

198
00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:20.920
from using require. Takeaway number three, relentless execution. So, I showed you how to

199
00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:26.760
divide the work, how to identify the critical areas, and then just go and execute. Remove

200
00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:30.199
all these things that are affecting your application, and you will start seeing the results.

201
00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:39.880
Now, after a few months working on this project, reality hit. We were just so happy that we reduced

202
00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:45.160
the bundle size so much. We're happy that we're making improvements to the network. But then,

203
00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:49.079
despite all of this, we were not seeing the results that we expected.

204
00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:55.640
And it didn't make sense. I was looking at the real user metrics, and it didn't make sense.

205
00:19:55.640 --> 00:20:00.439
I was expecting a huge reduction, at least on that section for the JavaScript loading.

206
00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:04.040
And I could not see that, even though the bundle had been reduced by 90%.

207
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:10.760
So, what we had to do was just keep going and dig deeper and re-evaluate what we were doing

208
00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:15.400
and verify the data until we discovered something.

209
00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:21.800
There was a render-blocking process in the main process of Electron that was competing with our

210
00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:28.680
resources in the renderer, making that download and parsing of JavaScript sequential. So, you can

211
00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:34.280
imagine we still have a lot of JavaScript, and then the main process is completely blocking,

212
00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:39.400
stopping our JavaScript execution. Which meant that it doesn't matter how much bundle size

213
00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:44.199
reductions we have done, actually, they were just still waiting because they were blocked by the

214
00:20:44.199 --> 00:20:49.239
main thread. And there is one main rule on Electron, and it's, do not block the main thread.

215
00:20:49.239 --> 00:20:56.040
That's the golden rule. And we found this, and it was a moment of, okay, all of the improvements

216
00:20:56.040 --> 00:21:01.319
that we have done so far are now starting to show because we removed this particular bug that was

217
00:21:01.319 --> 00:21:07.640
affecting the main thread. And how we found it, we found it using this tool called Tracing.

218
00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:12.920
And you can use it with Cron Dev Tools as well. But this Tracing is for the main process for Electron.

219
00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:19.239
So, this is not the main process, the Tracing for the web app, this is for the main

220
00:21:19.239 --> 00:21:24.920
process of Electron. And we realized with this that we could find the place where it was blocking

221
00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:31.959
the render process. Now, it takes me to the advanced techniques. So, we have done the basics, we have

222
00:21:31.959 --> 00:21:37.640
done all of these things, but this exercise of doing the Tracing in Electron made us realize that there

223
00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:42.439
was more that we could do. There was more performance that we could extract. And we discovered this

224
00:21:42.439 --> 00:21:48.359
called the five-byte bytecode caching, the full bytecode caching. And the full bytecode caching

225
00:21:48.359 --> 00:21:54.359
is an optimization that we could implement with our service worker, where you can just basically

226
00:21:54.359 --> 00:22:01.400
pre-compile all your JavaScript. So, JavaScript compiles on demand just in time. But with the

227
00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:07.880
service worker, we could just, at the install event, we generate the full code cache. So, these scripts

228
00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:13.719
were pre-compiled. I did not compile in a lazy manner, but install everything at once. Now, we

229
00:22:13.719 --> 00:22:18.520
could do that because we had our service worker. That improved our launch performance from the

230
00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:25.000
Electron side and the co-loading even further. There were other techniques and advanced performance

231
00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:30.599
techniques that we tried, but they gave us different results. For example, we tried with v8

232
00:22:30.599 --> 00:22:35.000
snapshots. I don't know if anyone is using v8 snapshots for the main process in Electron,

233
00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:42.680
but it was a very tricky and brittle process. We got some improvements out of the v8 snapshots,

234
00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:46.920
but we decided to go with just the full bytecode caching because it was more predictable.

235
00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:55.640
So, takeaway number four, adapt and iterate. We had to look at the metrics, we had to look

236
00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:01.079
at the results, and then figure out what things were not working, and adapt, and then continue

237
00:23:01.079 --> 00:23:09.000
iterating until we got the result. Now, the last thing is organizational challenges. I mean, we are

238
00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:14.839
developers, we like a really good technical challenge, but at the same time, most of the

239
00:23:14.839 --> 00:23:19.239
issues that we had were also because of organizational challenges. They were not purely

240
00:23:19.239 --> 00:23:25.640
technical. We had issues like we tried to have a performance team to go and fix the performance

241
00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:30.359
while the rest of the company was shipping features, and the problem is this small focus

242
00:23:30.359 --> 00:23:36.520
team didn't have all the tools to go and fix the problem. We also had a lack of prioritization.

243
00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:42.280
Performance, we knew we had a problem, but nobody just started saying, hey, let's go and fix it,

244
00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:47.800
which also generates a lack of ownership. Performance is not my problem, it's a problem

245
00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:52.280
with the performance team, so I don't care about performance of the features when I start adding

246
00:23:52.280 --> 00:24:01.319
more features. But our CEO, after he brought this laptop, he just said, right, this is enough.

247
00:24:01.319 --> 00:24:06.119
Performance is everybody's problem, and he brought the entire company together, and he gave us a

248
00:24:06.119 --> 00:24:12.839
mandate to go and fix performance. So our company was focused for a few months, where we just

249
00:24:12.839 --> 00:24:19.239
focused every single developer, every single product team on fixing performance, and that was our goal,

250
00:24:19.239 --> 00:24:27.400
and that's what we went to do. So takeaway number five is focus and prioritization. You need to

251
00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:33.479
bring the company together, bring the different developers from different teams, and come up with

252
00:24:33.479 --> 00:24:40.359
a plan and get executed as one team. It is not just one team's responsibility of performance,

253
00:24:40.359 --> 00:24:45.160
every single developer should be responsible for the performance of the application.

254
00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:53.079
So the results for Postman were incredible. We went from 36 seconds at P90 for most of the

255
00:24:53.079 --> 00:25:00.760
slowest devices to just under 12.5 seconds for the slowest devices. And for Mac, it was even better.

256
00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:04.040
Our application starts between one and two seconds, depending on the machine.

257
00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:10.280
So this is what months of work and months of iteration achieved if you come together.

258
00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:15.880
Thank you very much. So my name is Ruben again. Here are my links if you want to follow me and ask

259
00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:22.280
any questions. Cheers.
